To convert from rectangular coordinates to polar coordinates we use the formulas $$\begin{cases} x = r\cos(\theta) \\ y = r\sin(\theta)\end{cases}.$$We can find $r$ as follows: \begin{align*}
x &= r\cos(\theta) \\
0 &= r\cos(\theta)\\
r &= \boxed{0}.
\end{align*}

We can then solve for $\theta$ using the formula $$y = r\sin(\theta).$$Substituting our values for $r$ and $x,$ we get $$3 = (0)(\sin(\theta)) \implies \sin(\theta) = 1.$$The angle whose sine is one is $\frac{\pi}{2},$ so the solution will be $\left(0, \frac{\pi}{2}\right).$ Thus, our final answer is $\boxed{(0, \frac{\pi}{2})}.$

Let $A(x_1,y_1,z_1)$ and $B(x_2,y_2,z_2)$ be two points on each of the above lines respectively.
Then we have:
\begin{align*}
