We begin with our subtraction problem:
$$\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c}
& & 5 & 8_{9}\\
- & & 1 & 8_{9}\\
\cline{2-5}
\end{array}$$

Since we cannot subtract $8$ from $5$, we borrow one unit from the left and add it to $5$. We now have $14$ minus $8$ which is equal to $6$. Our problem then becomes:
$$\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c}
& & 6 & 7_{9}\\
- & & 1 & 8_{9}\\
\cline{2-5}
\end{array}$$

We cannot subtract $8$ from $7$ either, so we again borrow one unit and add it to $7$. This gives us $16$ minus $8$ which equals $8$. Our problem thus reduces to:
$$\begin{array}{c@{}c@{\;}c@{}c@{}c}
& & 1 & 6_{9}\\
- & & 1 & 8_{9}\\
\cline{2-5}
\end{array}$$

Finally, we can subtract $1$ from $1$ which leaves us with $\boxed{06_9}$ or $6_9$.

