The slope of any line connecting two distinct points with integer coordinates can be written as $\frac{a}{b}$ where $a,b\in\mathbb Z$, so we want to find the largest such ratio. The lines through the given squares have slopes that are either $\pm 1$ or $\pm 2$, so let's see if there is a way to make them all equal to one another. If they were parallel, then their $x$-intercepts would have to be the same; but the only point that satisfies both conditions is $(6,2)$, and since this is not on the left side of the first square, it cannot be the intercept. This means the lines must intersect at some point other than $(6,2)$. One such possibility is for these lines to meet at $(6,2)$; but this violates our assumption about how the lines should look, so it's impossible. We will instead consider a scenario where the lines cross each other, say at $(c,d)$. Then the distance between the intersection point and each endpoint of the corresponding line segment is the length of the legs of a right triangle whose hypotenuse is the distance from $(0,0)$ to $(c,d)$, the center of the second square. In order for this to work out, the lengths of the legs must add up to 2, and they must also be integers. So the possibilities are $(d,c)=(1,1)$ and $(1,3)$ or $(3,1)$ and $(3,3)$; but neither works because the distances do not add up to 2. However, if we choose $(c,d)=(-1,-1)$, then the leg lengths add up to 2, and the area of the rectangle formed by the sides of those triangles gives us the desired maximum: $$\text{area}=\frac{(1)(1)}{2}+\frac{(1)(1)}{2}=\frac{1}{2}+\frac{1}{2}=1,$$and thus the slope of the line is $\boxed{\frac{1}{1}}$.

