The two foci of the hyperbola are located at $(10,2)$ and $(-16,2),$ so the center is at $\left(\frac{10-16}{2},2\right) = (-3,2).$
Since $|PF_1-PF_2|=24,$ we have $|PF_1|-|PF_2|=24,$ which means that the distance from any point on the hyperbola to one focus is always 24 more than the distance from it to the other focus.
In other words, the difference between the distances from any point on the hyperbola to the foci is constant and equal to the length of the transverse axis of the hyperbola.
Thus, the lengths of the transverse and conjugate axes must both be 24. Since the vertices of the hyperbola lie along the major axis, the vertex with coordinate $(-3,-2)$ lies on the x-axis; thus, the coordinates of its center are $(-3,0).$
Therefore, the standard form of the hyperbola's equation is $\frac{(x+3)^2}{24^2}-\frac{y^2}{24^2}=1$, or $\frac{x^2}{576}-\frac{y^2}{576}=1.$
Hence, $h=-3,\ k=0,\ a=24,\ b=24,$ so $h+k+a+b=\boxed{-3}$.

