Since $P(x)$ is a quadratic polynomial, we can write it as $P(x) = ax^2 + bx + c$. We know that $a > 0$, since otherwise there would not be an upper bound on $P(x)$ for all real $x$. Since this is also true for the lower bound, we have $b < 0$. Furthermore, because the left hand side of the inequality is always positive, we must have $c > 0$. Now consider the following system of equations: $$\begin{cases} P(11) = 181 \\ x^2 - 2x + 2 \le P(x) \le 2x^2 - 4x + 3.\end{cases}$$This gives us the equations $$\begin{cases} 121a + 11b + c = 181 \\ x^2 - 2x + 2 \le ax^2 + bx + c \\ 2x^2 - 4x + 3 \ge ax^2 + bx + c.\end{cases}$$From the second equation, subtracting $ax^2 + bx + c$ from both sides gives $$x^2 - (a+2)x + (2-b-c) \le 0,$$so $(a+2)^2-4(2-b-c)\le 0$. From the third equation, subtracting $ax^2 + bx + c$ from both sides gives $$2x^2 - (4-a-b-c)x + (3-b-c) \ge 0,$$so $(4-a-b-c)^2-4(3-b-c)\ge 0.$ Expanding these two inequalities yields $$\begin{align*}
(a+2)^2-4(2-b-c)&=a^2 + 4a + 4 - 8 + 4b + 4c\\
&=(a+2)^2 + 4(b+c) - 4\\
&\le 0,
\end{align*}$$and similarly $$\begin{align*}
(4-a-b-c)^2-4(3-b-c)&=16 - 8a - 8b - 8c + a^2 + b^2 + 2ab + c^2 - 12 + 4b + 4c\\
&=(a+2)^2 + (b+c)^2 + 2(ab - 4).\end{align*}$$We want to find values of $a,b,c$ such that $\sqrt{(a+2)^2 + 4(b+c)}\le 0$ and $-(a+2)^2 - (b+c)^2 - 4ab + 4\le 0$. This last condition simplifies to $$(a+2)^2 + (b+c)^2 + 4ab\ge 0.$$Combining these conditions gives us $$\begin{aligned}(a+2)^2 + 4(b+c) &= 0\\ (a+2)^2 + (b+c)^2 + 4ab &= 0.\end{aligned}\tag{*}$$Note that $a^2 + 4(b+c) = -(a+2)^2 - (b+c)^2 - 4ab = -(a+2)^2 - (b+c)^2 - 4ab$ so $(*)$ is equivalent to $$(a+2)^2 + (b+c)^2 = 0,$$which means that $a=-2$, $b+c=0$. But then we have $$\begin{aligned}(-2+2)^2 + (b+c)^2 &= 0\\ (-2)^2 + (b+c)^2 &= 0,\end{aligned}$$which has no solution. Thus our assumption was incorrect and the correct answer is $P(16)=\boxed{-17}$, which can be obtained directly by plugging into the given bounds.
