We now want to calculate the probability of getting a sum of 7. To do so, we'll look at all possible ways to get a sum of 7 from one roll of the die. There are four possibilities: $(1,6)$, $(2,5)$, $(3,4)$, and $(4,3)$. For each possibility, we will then multiply by the probability of getting those specific outcomes. Note that there is only one way to obtain each outcome: either the first die shows the corresponding number or the second die shows the corresponding number.

For example, if we have a single roll of the die showing a 1, we can use the fact that the probability of getting an $f$ is $\tfrac{1}{3}$ to see that the probability of getting a sum of 7 is $$\tfrac{1}{3}\cdot\tfrac{1}{6}+\tfrac{1}{3}\cdot\tfrac{1}{6}=\tfrac{1}{9},$$ since we could also have gotten a 2 instead of a 1. In general, we can apply the same reasoning to any single roll of the die, leading us to conclude that the probability of getting a sum of 7 when rolling the die once is $$\tfrac{1}{3}\left(\tfrac{1}{6}+\tfrac{1}{6}\right)=\tfrac{1}{9}.$$

