\end{align*}

Therefore, we see that the leading coefficient of $h(f(x))$ is $x^{10}$, which implies that the leading coefficient of $h(x)$ must be $\frac{1}{x^{10}} = x^{-10}$ (because there's an inverse relationship between powers).
Thus, the degree of $h$ is $-10$.  Therefore, our answer is $\boxed{-10}$.

Note that since $A+B+C=\pi$, we have $B+C=\pi-A$.  Hence,
\begin{align*}
\sin B \sin C &= \sin(B+C)\cos(A-C) - \sin(A-B)\cos(C+A) \\
&= \sin(\pi-A)\cos(A-C) - \sin(-2C)\cos(2A).
\end{align*}Using $\sin(\pi-x)=\sin x$ and $\cos(\pi+x)=\cos x$ as well as the double angle formulas for sine and cosine, this simplifies to
\[\sin B \sin C = \sin A \cos A - \cos A \sin A = 0.\]Therefore, if $\sin A > 0$, then $\sin B \sin C < 0$, which is impossible.  So it must be the case that $\sin A = 0$.  But $\sin A=0$ only when $A=0^\circ$, but there can't be any such triangle.  Thus, the maximum value is $\boxed{0}$, achieved only when $A=B=C=60^\circ$.

