Let us examine what happens when $x$ equals each endpoint. When $x = 1$, we get $$|1-1|+|1-1.5|+|1-2|=0+0.5+1=\frac{3}{2}.$$When $x=1.5$, we get $$|1.5-1|+|1.5-1.5|+|1.5-2|=0.5+0+0.5=\boxed{\frac{3}{2}}.$$When $x=2$, we get $$|2-1|+|2-1.5|+|2-2|=1+0.5+0=\frac{3}{2},$$so this is also $\frac{3}{2}$ and so on for all other values of $x$. Therefore, it seems that our answer is always $\frac{3}{2}.$ We can prove this rigorously with calculus. Let us take the derivative of the function $$f(x)=|x-1|+|x-1.5|+|x-2|,$$which gives us $$\begin{aligned}
f'(x)&=\text{sgn}(x-1)+\text{sgn}(x-1.5)+\text{sgn}(x-2), \\
&=\left\{
\begin{array}{ll}
4 & x<1\\
3 & 1<x<1.5\\
2 & 1.5<x<2\\
1 & x>2.
\end{array}\right.\end{aligned}$$Since $f'$ changes from negative to positive at $x=1.5$, there must be some point between these two points where $f(x)$ has a minimum, which means that $f(1.5)=\frac{3}{2}.$

The key observation here is that if we draw perpendiculars from $B$ to $AM$ and $MC$, respectively, they will bisect $BM$ and $CM$. Let's call these points $X$ and $Y$. Since these perpendiculars also bisect $AB$ and $BC$, we get that triangles $AXB$ and $AYC$ are congruent. So we can conclude that $AX=CY$.
